hi my name is Dan Fletcher I'm a  professor of bioengineering and  biophysics at UC Berkeley and my lab  works on the cytoskeleton we're  interested in how forces sculpt cellular  organization and behavior but a little  over a year ago I wasn't doing that I  was taking some time to work at the  White House very different experience  from from working in lab and I went to  washington d.c out of an interest in  understanding why the taxpayers are  paying us to do research how public  policy makers are using the information  that we are generating and what is there  that we can do to improve that that  process and so it turned out to be a  tremendous experience I thoroughly  enjoyed the time there but it's great to  be back and in a research lab part of  the reason i enjoyed it is it exposed me  two very different people and very  different experiences than i have during  my normal career most of us haven't  really been outside of academia if  you've been in academia as a result you  don't really get a chance to understand  some of what motivates different parts  of the country so this was a chance to  get a little bit of our perspective on  that broad set of people so I wanted to  talk about a few of the experiences that  I had in DC but also try and identify a  few thoughts on how to be effective in  communicating research ideas which is a  central role for scientists in  Washington DC so Washington DC is a  whole new world very different from  Berkeley where I live and very different  from most academic environments it's a  very formal place I had to wear a suit  and tie every day I had to go to  meetings on time it was a very unique  experience I always even told to get a  haircut and that was by karl rove and  Matt and I didn't get a haircut but it  taught you  of things about how people organized and  how people decide what policies are  worthwhile there's not an experiment you  can do to decide that policy it comes  from conversations from interactions and  that's why cocktail parties in  Washington DC are so important they're  like our conferences there's no poster  exhibit but there is a chance to  exchange information so what does a  scientist do in Washington DC well my  goal in part was to be a link to  research labs active research labs that  are producing this information that the  taxpayers are paying for and to provide  some insight on how that process works  and hopefully some specific knowledge  that would be useful in policy policy  decisions I came to Washington DC as  part of a program called the White House  Fellows Program and it's a program  that's been around since about nineteen  sixty-five and it brings early career  professionals from a wide variety of  disciplines to Washington DC for an  experience there and my class of fellows  was a group of 14 people and a fantastic  and varied group of people including  doctors and lawyers and business people  and people from the military a fabulous  experience and a wonderful group of  people so about half of my time in  Washington DC was spent working with  this group of people and we met with  over a hundred different speakers over  the course of the time there including  President the Vice President various  cabinet secretaries and other  influential people such as colonists and  activists and so in those meetings we  got a chance to discuss a wide variety  of ish issues and it was very  educational process the other half of my  time was spent at the White House Office  of Science and Technology Policy working  together with the science advisor as  well as a variety of other people in  that office and we worked on issues that  ranged from life sciences to biosecurity  to health technology men each day more  issues would be coming in  or you would be trying to push other  topics forward and many of the decisions  that need to be made were what do we do  next what is the right role for the  government in a particular topic whether  its stem cell research or the  requirement for certain health  information to be made available um one  thing I observed right away was that  Washington DC is a very busy place a  very fast-paced environment there's not  a lot of time for contemplation things  happen very quickly and people work very  hard so have a great deal of respect for  the civil servants who form the core of  the government so in this process that  is so fast-paced what is the role for  science so it was really exciting to see  that everywhere I went and the people I  talked with science is something that is  broadly supported Democrats Republicans  the White House the Congress science is  seen as a real key to addressing  national problems and national  priorities and so that was wonderful to  see what was less clear was how is the  information that we're producing getting  into these environments it's to some  extent through advisory councils to some  extent through news stories but there  weren't direct routes to figure out what  does the future look like and that's  that was surprising to me it was also  surprising and maybe a little  disheartening to realize that science is  not the answer that is science doesn't  address a policy issue completely it  provides a lot of the background  information but at some point someone  has to be a decision-maker someone has  to say this is how we're going to do it  not this way and many times that's a  value judgment that's not a scientific  judgment and as a result science is  playing a role up to a point and after  that it's it's balancing of various  interest and balancing those interests  is the great challenge of any system of  government and getting a perspective on  where science plays a role was was a  very rewarding part of this experience  so this brings me to me  the main point and that is how can we a  scientist be effective in Washington DC  what is our role I think there are five  things that we can do we can do one is  we need to play a role in synthesizing  ideas we are all experts in specific  areas but those specific areas and that  particular topic is only going to be  part of a solution and so if we can play  a role in synthesizing what we do  together what other people do to come up  with solutions that's going to be much  more effective than than just describing  the work that we're doing individually  so being able to synthesize being a  little broad is very important and being  effective in in Washington DC second is  recognizing the diversity of interests  people come to Washington DC with lots  of different polls on them and  understanding who's in favor of an idea  who's against an idea is a critical part  of moving a topic forward third and  something I didn't realize until a few  months into this program was that famous  people are just people even if you're  sitting in the Oval Office or having  lunch with colon Powell these are these  are people they've done amazing things  but they benefit from the same sort of  hard questions the same sort of critical  assessment that we do in improving  ourselves in our work and so I think  that's an important thing to remember  when you're interacting with government  officials is they adhere to the same  rules of logic that we do fourth is come  with solutions not just with problems  the most effective way to make progress  in Washington DC is to have a solution  and if you simply point out a problem  with the way funding is done or the lack  of funding in a particular area that's  not a solution there are lots of  problems the government deals with it's  much more exciting and much more  productive to come with a solution how  to move forward on that topic and lastly  maybe most importantly is that we as  scientists have a perspective on where  our fields are going and that's the  vision we need to share with  the policymakers in Washington DC we  have ideas we have a perspective that  they aren't going to have the time or  the ability to to have and we need to  come up with ways of sharing that  perspective a quote i like that relates  to this is one piece of advice given to  wayne gretzky the the ice hockey player  when he was told to not skate to where  the puck is but skate to where the puck  is going to be i think that's the big  challenge in government devising  policies that are going to be  appropriate for where the world's going  to be in then 10 20 years and we're the  ones who have an idea where science is  going to be in that amount of time so we  need to play a role in conveying that  idea so it was a wonderful experience to  be in DC but I'm wonderfully happy to be  back in berkeley back in lab and  enjoying the west coast thank you  you
 

